  "scenario": "A manufacturer of smart home devices (thermostats, cameras, door locks) uses embedded firmware with limited RAM (64KB) and flash memory (256KB). The devices use ECC P-256 for secure boot and TLS 1.2 for cloud communication. The manufacturer's cloud backend uses RSA-2048. The devices must remain operational for 10 years. The manufacturer wants to migrate to PQC but is constrained by the small memory footprint. NIST's ML-KEM requires more memory and CPU than ECC P-256. The manufacturer has a budget of $150,000 and a change freeze from Nov 15 to Jan 15. The cloud backend is hosted on AWS (which will support ML-KEM in 2027).",
